The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
Initially, the $i$-th alien has $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult after $i$ years.
When the $i$-th alien becomes an adult (at year $i$), every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the $i$-th alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
The aliens are indexed $1$ to $N$.
Let's analyze the process.
At year $i$, the $i$-th alien turns adult.
The condition "every adult who has at least one stone" refers to aliens who have already become adults.
Alien $j$ becomes an adult at year $j$. So at year $i$, aliens $1, 2, \ldots, i-1$ are adults.
Wait, let's re-read carefully.
"The $i$-th alien ... will become an adult exactly $i$ years later."
"When someone becomes an adult ... every adult who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone as a congratulatory gift to the alien who has just become an adult."
So, at year $i$, alien $i$ becomes an adult. The set of adults present at this moment includes aliens $1, 2, \ldots, i-1$.
Wait, does alien $i$ count as an adult *before* receiving the gift? The text says "to the alien who has just become an adult". Usually, this implies the newly adult doesn't give to themselves, but receives from existing adults.
Let's check the sample explanation.
Sample 1: $N=4$, $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
